  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nWIDESPREAD PRECIPITATION THROUGH TUESDAY:\n\nMoisture advection from tropical storm activity in the Eastern \nPacific will bring anomalous moisture to our region through at \nleast midweek. PWAT anomalies will be upwards of 200-250% of \nnormal for a good chunk of the forecast area, especially south \nof I-70. This means widespread precipitation chances for much of\nthe region.\n \nA closed upper-level low digs southward through California on \ntoday, and then across the Desert Southwest on Tuesday and \nWednesday. A jet streak aloft and energy from a trough axis will\nwork in tandem to create lift for continuous showers and storms\nfor the rest of the day. As the low continues to track just \nsouth of the AZ/NM/UT/CO state lines on Tuesday, drier periods \nare possible with the best forcing skirting south of the \nforecast area. However, The potential for moderate to heavy \nprecipitation will continue to pose a threat for the Southern \nSan Juans and adjacent valleys. \n\nThe potential for prolonged periods of rainfall, including in \nareas that have recently received substantial rainfall, poses \nflooding concerns through Wednesday. As a result, a Flood Watch \nis in effect for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado through \n6AM Wednesday. Please see the Hydrology Section below for more \ninformation on those flooding concerns.\n\nMOISTURE BEGINS TO EXIT THE REGION WEDNESDAY:\n\nBy Wednesday evening, the elevated moisture and closed low will\nmove east of the Rocky Mountains, leading to drier weather for \nmuch of the forecast area. \n\nHigh pressure looks to drive the remainder of the seven-day \nforecast. As such, predominately dry conditions with \ntemperatures running around 5 degrees above normal for early \nOctober can be expected late week through next weekend.",
